IS256 × ica locus — phase-variation animation · licensing
Copyright (c) 2026 Sean P Nair

This project is released under two licences, because the same files hold two
different kinds of work: a piece of software, and the teaching material it
draws.


SOFTWARE — MIT License          (full text in LICENSE-MIT)

    The HTML, CSS and JavaScript considered as source code: the rendering
    engine and render(t) state machine, the timeline and easing machinery,
    the chapter/scrubber transport controls, the five short synthesized
    audio cues, and the page layout.

    Fork it, adapt it, build your own animations on it, commercially or
    otherwise. Keep the copyright notice. No attribution beyond that notice
    is required.


CONTENT — Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0)
                                (details in LICENSE-CC-BY-4.0)

    The expressive material: the written captions, chapter labels, gene and
    protein annotations, figure text and study-guide prose — including where
    that text sits inside the source files; the visual design of the
    animation and the student guide; and any still image, screen recording,
    frame grab or other capture of them, including is256_ica_loop.mp4, the
    screenshots in docs/ and the prose of this README.

    Share it and adapt it, for any purpose including commercially, but give
    appropriate credit, link to the licence, and say if you changed it.


THE SHORT VERSION

    Reusing the machinery needs no credit. Reusing the words or the pictures
    does. If you are unsure which licence covers a particular use, crediting
    the author satisfies both.


ATTRIBUTION

    A suggested form, for a slide, a handout or a caption:

        "IS256 × ica locus — phase-variation animation" by Sean P Nair, CC BY 4.0
        (link to wherever you host it)

    If you modified the animation or the guide, please say so, so that any
    errors you introduce are not attributed to the original.


A NOTE ON THE SPLIT

    The boundary is between the program and what the program expresses. Code
    that draws an operon is software and is MIT. The picture of the operon it
    produces, and the sentence explaining what IS256 is doing, are content
    and are CC BY 4.0. In practice: a developer forking the engine is working
    under MIT; a teacher putting a frame, caption or guide page into a
    lecture is working under CC BY 4.0.
